<section id="controls">
  <h2>The controls</h2>

  <h3>Repository selector</h3>
  <p>The dashboard is your primary view within Distilled. The page shows you metrics for one repository at a time; Distilled allows you to focus on the delivery health of a codebase and its pipeline, but not of an average across them. Switch repositories via the selector at the top right of the page.</p>

  <h3>Time window</h3>
  <p>Distilled offers you three time windows: <strong>30 days</strong>, <strong>90 days</strong> and <strong>6 months</strong>.</p>
  <p>Note that when you first start using Distilled the only time window available will be 30 days. Over time as more data is collected the longer time windows will become available.</p>

  <h3>Freshness indicator</h3>
  <p>Underneath the repository name in the top left you will see a short overview of when metrics were last recomputed and how many days of data exist. If the status dot is red, this means the batch metric calculations have not been run recently and you should treat the data as slightly behind. (Distilled will catch up quickly.) More detail is available here: <a href="/docs/data-quality">Data Quality</a>.</p>

  <h3>Profile menu</h3>
  <p>In the top right of the page you will find the profile menu. Switch or create workspaces and, if you're an owner, reach the <strong>Team</strong>, <strong>Repositories</strong> and <strong>Deployment Tracking</strong> settings.</p>
</section>

<section id="key-metrics">
  <h2>Key metrics</h2>
  <p>The top row answers "how are we doing right now?" with five metrics. Each reflects the selected repository and time window, except the open-PR figures, which are always current state.</p>

  <div class="docs-rows">
    <div class="docs-row">
      <strong>Deployment Frequency (deploys / week)</strong>
      <p>Production ships per week across the window.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-row">
      <strong>Lead Time (median: merge to production)</strong>
      <p>How long merged code waits before users get it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-row">
      <strong>PR Cycle Time (median: PR open to merge)</strong>
      <p>How long the review-and-iterate loop takes.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-row">
      <strong>Throughput (PRs / engineer / month)</strong>
      <p>Merged pull requests, normalised by the number of distinct authors.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-row">
      <strong>Open PRs (live and draft)</strong>
      <p>How much work is in flight right now.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="trends">
  <h2>Trends</h2>
  <p>Four charts answer the question "are things getting better or worse?". Each chart has an <strong>info</strong> button explaining what it plots.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Deployment Frequency</strong>: deployments per day across the current time window.</li>
    <li><strong>Lead Time</strong>: weekly median and 75th percentile in hours.</li>
    <li><strong>PR Cycle Time</strong>: weekly median and 75th percentile in hours.</li>
    <li><strong>PR Ageing</strong>: the age distribution of pull requests open right now.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Headline numbers are computed live over exactly the window you selected; the weekly chart series are recomputed on a schedule. That's why a chart can lag a headline figure by up to an hour.</p>
</section>

